# The failure branches, which the obligation audit found nothing testing.
#
# Every rule in the specs has a failure obligation as well as a success one,
# and 68 of them had no test beside them. These are the ones where the gap
# was real rather than structural: paths that select nothing, conditions that
# are not conditions, and calls that cannot be made.

# --- Paths that go wrong -------------------------------------

--- id failures/a-path-through-a-word-with-no-value-blames-the-word
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule PathEvaluates
--- requires control
--- note The error must name the word rather than the path. Reporting an
--- note invalid path here would send the reader looking at the wrong end of
--- note the expression, which is the whole reason no-value and not-defined
--- note are kept apart in the first place.
--- code
do [nothing/here]
--- error script no-value

--- id failures/selecting-a-field-an-object-does-not-have-raises
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule PathSelectsNextSegment
--- requires control series
--- note An object either has the field or the code is wrong, so this raises
--- note rather than giving NONE. The contrast is the next entry.
--- code
holder: [a b c]
holder/nosuchthing
--- error script invalid-path

--- id failures/selecting-past-the-end-of-a-series-gives-none
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule PathSelectsNextSegment
--- requires series
--- note A series has positions that hold nothing, so reading one is not a
--- note mistake. This asymmetry with objects is asserted from reasoning and
--- note is still waiting on a real R3 to confirm.
--- code
holder: [a b c]
holder/9
--- result
_

--- id failures/selecting-by-position-works
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule PathSelectsNextSegment
--- requires series
--- code
holder: [a b c]
holder/2
--- result
b

--- id failures/a-path-cannot-select-from-a-number
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule PathSelectsNextSegment
--- requires control
--- code
count: 42
count/field
--- error script invalid-path

--- id failures/a-set-path-into-something-that-is-not-an-object-raises
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule SetPathAssignsFollowingValue
--- requires control
--- code
count: 42
count/field: 1
--- error script invalid-path

# --- An unset is a condition, and it is true -----------------

--- id failures/if-handed-an-unset-takes-the-branch
--- origin Rebol's src/include/sys-value.h, IS_FALSE
--- requires control
--- note IS_FALSE is `IS_NONE(v) || (IS_LOGIC(v) && !VAL_LOGIC(v))`. It never
--- note asks whether a value is unset, thus an unset is not false and every
--- note conditional takes its true branch for one.
--- note
--- note These three entries said the opposite and named JEBOL as their
--- note origin, which the corpus README warns about: an entry that records
--- note what this implementation happens to do proves nothing.
--- code
nothing: does [exit]
if nothing ["taken"]
--- result
"taken"

--- id failures/either-handed-an-unset-takes-the-true-branch
--- origin Rebol's src/core/n-control.c, REBNATIVE(either)
--- requires control
--- note `argnum = IS_FALSE(D_ARG(1)) ? 3 : 2` -- an unset is not false, thus
--- note the second argument is the one that runs.
--- code
nothing: does [exit]
either nothing ["true branch"] ["false branch"]
--- result
"true branch"

--- id failures/not-handed-an-unset-answers-false
--- origin Rebol's src/core/n-control.c, REBNATIVE(not)
--- requires control
--- note NOT of anything that is not false is false, and an unset is not
--- note false.
--- code
nothing: does [exit]
not nothing
--- result
#(false)

# --- Calls that cannot be made -------------------------------

--- id failures/calling-something-that-is-not-a-function
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule ApplicationResolvesGathering
--- requires control
--- note A path that lands on a number cannot be called, and saying so is
--- note better than silently producing the number.
--- code
do [count: 42 count/1]
--- error script invalid-path

--- id failures/arithmetic-on-a-block-raises
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- requires control
--- code
add 10 [a b]
--- error script expect-arg

--- id failures/arithmetic-on-a-string-raises
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 4.7
--- requires control
--- code
multiply "text" 2
--- error script expect-arg

--- id failures/a-loop-count-must-be-a-number
--- origin JEBOL spec/natives.allium
--- requires control
--- code
loop "three" [1]
--- error script expect-arg

--- id failures/foreach-needs-something-to-walk
--- origin JEBOL spec/natives.allium
--- requires control
--- code
foreach item 42 [item]
--- error script cannot-use

# --- Assignment that cannot happen ---------------------------

--- id failures/a-set-word-at-the-end-of-a-block
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule SetWordEvaluates
--- requires control
--- code
do [total:]
--- error script need-value

--- id failures/assigning-to-a-protected-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 5.7
--- requires control
--- code
kept: "original"
protect 'kept
kept: "replacement"
--- error script locked-word

--- id failures/an-operator-with-nothing-on-its-left
--- origin JEBOL spec/eval.allium, rule WordEvaluates
--- requires control op
--- note An operator takes its first argument from what came before it, so
--- note one at the start of a block has nothing to take. R3 gives this its
--- note own id, distinct from the one for a missing right-hand argument.
--- code
do [+ 1]
--- error script no-op-arg

--- id failures/an-operator-with-nothing-on-its-right
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires control op
--- note The other side of the same operator, and a different id: running
--- note out of block is no-arg, whichever kind of call ran out.
--- code
do [1 +]
--- error script no-arg
